  "TAS:moonah:January": "Summer is established. Tomatoes, beans, cucumbers and zucchini produce heavily. Pick constantly.\r\n\r\nSow autumn crops now: carrots, beetroot, broccoli, kale, cabbage, silverbeet in warm soil. Chill lettuce seed or sow in shade.\r\n\r\nWater deeply morning. Mulch heavily while ground is warm and moist.",
  "TAS:moonah:February": "Garden feels summery but light softens. Plant brassicas from January seedlings—broccoli, cabbage, kale establish well.\r\n\r\nContinue sowing beetroot, carrots, silverbeet, Asian greens for autumn.\r\n\r\nHot winds can dry beds. Deep watering before heat better than rescuing wilted plants.",
  "TAS:moonah:March": "Autumn arrives quickly. Nights cool sharply, first light frosts possible late month.\r\n\r\nPlant garlic. Sow spinach, peas, broad beans, Asian greens as soil cools.\r\n\r\nGrowth slows. Mulch for frost buffering. Keep fleece nearby for seedlings.",
  "TAS:moonah:April": "True autumn planting season. Soil cooling but warm enough for hardy crops.\r\n\r\nPlant brassicas, leeks, onions, lettuce, silverbeet. Sow spinach, peas, beans directly. Garlic succeeds through month.\r\n\r\nClear summer crops promptly. Empty beds need compost or green manure.",
  "TAS:moonah:May": "Winter begins. Frost grows regular, soil stays wetter, growth slows.\r\n\r\nBroad beans, spinach, silverbeet, garlic and hardy greens produce through mild spells.\r\n\r\nAvoid walking wet soil. Add compost and seaweed to empty beds.",
  "TAS:moonah:June": "Cold settles fully. Hardy vegetables continue—spinach, beans, silverbeet, brassicas crop through milder periods.\r\n\r\nProtection matters. Young plants need shelter from wind as much as frost. Mulch root crops.",
  "TAS:moonah:July": "Usually coldest month. Very little grows fast. Maintain winter crops rather than expect heavy harvests.\r\n\r\nUse month for planning: order seed, repair beds, sharpen tools, plan spacing. Spring mistakes happen in July through impatience.",
  "TAS:moonah:August": "Early spring stirs though winter hasn't released. Bright days come but frosts stay frequent.\r\n\r\nStart tomatoes, capsicums, eggplants indoors. Sow lettuce, onions, brassicas under protection.\r\n\r\nResist rushing. Patient spring gardens overtake early damaged ones by November.",
  "TAS:moonah:September": "Spring underway though frost still arrives on calm nights.\r\n\r\nPlant onions, leeks, brassicas, silverbeet, lettuces. Sow carrots, beetroot, parsnips, peas. Successive sowing beats one glut.\r\n\r\nTomatoes, capsicums stay protected unless late month in warm spots.",
  "TAS:moonah:October": "Everything accelerates. Soil warms, daylight stretches, growth shows daily.\r\n\r\nEarly October frost surprises. By late month, tomatoes, beans, zucchini, pumpkins plant safely in sheltered gardens.\r\n\r\nDirect sowing productive. Beans, beetroot, carrots respond quickly.",
  "TAS:moonah:November": "Great planting month. Soil warm, frost risk low, everything grows confidently.\r\n\r\nPlant tomatoes, cucumbers, pumpkins, zucchini, beans. Capsicums and eggplants need warmest spots.\r\n\r\nMulch deeply. Consistent moisture prevents bitterness and blossom-end rot.",
